From a936ef1178d69e4a27c02d97d77ca701bbf04f81 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: howl <howeili@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 10:29:59 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] sm89: the dual-scope gather in three staged kernels
 (SGLANG_SM89_ATTN_STAGED)

The unsplit dual-scope kernel walks one row's top-k on four CTAs (BLOCK_H 16, 64 heads),
about 4 of an RTX 4080's 80 SMs. In an online softmax only the running maximum and the fp32
folds carry order, not the MMAs, so with SGLANG_SM89_ATTN_STAGED=1 up to eight rows run as
three kernels: the scores and the probabilities on every (head block, token block), then the
output on every (head block, tile), folding the token blocks in order. Every value goes
through the same operations in the same order as the unsplit kernel, so the output is bit for
bit its output. It reads the V4, V41 and V41_FP4 caches. Those rows leave
SGLANG_SM89_ATTN_SPLITK=auto's split-K for it; an explicit 2, 4 or 8 still wins.

Checked by howl9003 on an RTX 4080 SUPER, on their SM89 build with SGLANG_SM89_ATTN_SPLITK=0:
- against the unsplit kernel: 0 of 3,000 fuzzed calls (half with NaN and infinity), 0 of
  1,000 on V41 / V41_FP4 caches, 11,496 scored positions of a model run identical;
- the gather 39.2 -> 20.5, 55.5 -> 26.4 and 19.6 -> 13.3 us a call under CUDA graph replay;
  decode, median of six workloads, 31.25 -> 32.0 tok/s; 35K prefill unchanged.
Not measured against SGLANG_SM89_ATTN_SPLITK=auto.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
